WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:08.700
Hello, my name is Kendra Godwin, and 
I am a 2016-2017 Associate Fellow

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.500
at the National Library of Medicine, 
referred to here in this presentation as NLM.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.920
This recording was created as part of my fall project,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:21.220
in partnership with the Exhibition Program of 
the History of Medicine Division.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.880
I produced this to serve as a guide for individuals 
who are developing companion programming

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.659
in preparation for hosting the exhibit,
"Confronting Violence: Improving Women’s Lives."

00:00:31.659 --> 00:00:36.619
While this is my primary audience, I hope
this presentation inspires you

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.800
with ideas for whatever project you’re mounting.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.740
In this video, I will walk you through four sections.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.580
I will introduce the traveling exhibit
"Confronting Violence,"

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.980
the reasoning behind companion programming,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.380
experiences and examples from
"Confronting Violence" hosting institutions,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.720
and three of my own programming ideas for you.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:07.140
"Confronting Violence" is a traveling exhibit
curated by Dr. Catherine Jacquet, and produced by NLM.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.600
Told through images and records, it explores

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.480
the critical role that nurses
in the 20th century played in

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.060
identifying, treating, and preventing domestic violence.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.420
The traveling element, which you will be hosting
at your institution, is comprised of

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.840
six free-standing banners, seen here on this slide.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.380
These panels simply and evocatively communicate
the themes of "Confronting Violence."

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:39.000
In addition to this exhibit, there is a companion
website with more information, a Digital Gallery,

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:44.389
and resources for education and further study
that include related resources at NLM.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.480
This is designed to support hosts as they
prepare for the promotion of the exhibit and

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.520
the planning of related activities and programming.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.860
The central question that has driven the creation of this recording is:
How can NLM better support

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:05.000
the efforts of a hosting institution to reach and
meet the needs of its community members?

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.860
Related questions also include: How might
the community members at or brought together

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.480
by your institution respond to "Confronting
Violence," what stories might you want to tell,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:20.880
and how best could NLM resources be introduced
and integrated to further learning and conversation?

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.760
"Confronting Violence" covers an important topic
and can inspire meaningful thought and collaboration

00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:31.900
at your institution, and NLM wants to assist
in helping you engage beyond just the traveling

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.160
exhibit and its promotional materials.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.180
The companion programming ideas I have developed
and will present here shortly are meant to

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:45.270
provide a comfortable starting point for individuals
to explore NLM databases and other resources.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.960
Before beginning this project, I thought it
was critical to speak with institutions that

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:57.290
have hosted, are hosting, or will host "Confronting
Violence," in order to understand their reasons

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:02.780
for selecting the exhibit, their actual or
anticipated experiences, and any supplementary

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.280
activities or programs they had planned.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.920
I sent out emails to six individuals who were
exhibit contacts, heard back from five, and

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.170
was able to collect insight from four.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.260
These individuals are as follows:

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.440
Aphrodite Bodycomb, Associate Director for
Administration and Operations, was hosting

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:26.300
"Confronting Violence" at the time of our communication,
from September to November of 2016.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:33.580
Lauri Fennell, Reference &amp; Patron Services
Librarian, hosted from October to December of 2015.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.870
Beverly Murphy, Assistant Director of Communications
&amp; Web Content, and Hospital Nursing Liaison

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.820
for the Duke Health System, is scheduled to
host from May to June of 2017.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:49.140
And Judy Stribling, Manager of
the Myra Mahon Patient Resource Center,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.280
hosted from February to March of 2016.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.880
Common themes emerged from their responses.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:01.260
Reasons for hosting this exhibit include believing
the topic to be timely, of personal interest,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.800
important, or important but not given enough exposure.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.420
Individuals also felt that it was suited to

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.500
or could complement their health sciences programs.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:15.480
They wrote that the panels are informative,
appeal to various groups across their campus,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.420
and that the theme can be used to make
connections across multiple campus units.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.060
There was also mention of foot traffic, 
and wanting something present in

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.440
a public space to engage visitors.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.240
Individuals responded in a similar fashion to questions

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.740
regarding companion activities or programming,

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.080
that they try to complement exhibits with
their own efforts whenever possible,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.880
be it a display of items from special collections, or

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.800
a creative visualization of the number
of individuals impacted by violence daily.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:50.560
One individual did note that space, timing,
and manpower can be prohibitive to these endeavors.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:56.440
Recommendations from all included inviting
guest speakers, and scheduling panels or lectures.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.260
One involved a student who shared personal experience,

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:01.860
and another was a Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:07.760
Ideas for groups to invite include those who
deal with domestic violence on a daily basis,

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.180
like law enforcement, psychologists, and health care providers.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:16.470
There was also talk of involving Internal
Medicine health services, the Nursing School,

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:17.840
and the School of Social Work.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.980
I also thought it was important to understand

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.380
the community members of
the hosting institutions that responded.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.940
Starting from their websites, I researched what schools, populations,

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.720
and organizations they serve and what these services entail,

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.640
and I imagined what their community member needs might be,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.640
and what programming they might respond to.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.120
That insight is also included in my programming efforts.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:47.480
I want to return for a moment to the creative
visualization I mentioned a minute ago.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.740
I had the opportunity to visit
the Health Sciences &amp; Human Services Library

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.520
at the University of Maryland, Baltimore in December of 2016,

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.020
and the two photos on this slide are from that tour.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:04.080
Aphrodite Bodycomb and her exhibits team
developed a display using colored paper

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.200
to reflect the number of women impacted by
various violence within the span of only one hour.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.420
The case is full, as the second photo notes that “Every 9 seconds,

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:17.280
a woman in this country is a victim of a violent crime.”

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:21.720
While the "Confronting Violence" exhibit is
no longer hosted there, the display remains

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.000
centered in the foyer, the first thing people
see when they enter the library.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.120
The themes of "Confronting Violence" continue
to make a powerful impact.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.500
As the majority of the hosting institutions
interviewed for my project and within the

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:41.860
experience of the Exhibition Program are health
sciences libraries for higher education,

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.380
this is where I set my focus.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:50.060
The target audiences are faculty and students,
the public, and community organizations, and

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:55.400
I considered partnerships and NLM resources
with their interests and needs in mind.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:02.040
My first companion programming idea incorporates resources from

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:08.080
the National Information Center on Health Services Research
and Health Care Technology, known as NICHSR,

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.700
into the education of nursing students.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:18.020
NICHSR is an arm of NLM, and its mission is
to improve the collection, storage, analysis,

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.240
retrieval, and dissemination of health services research.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:29.260
Within NICHSR is the Health Services Research
Information Central portal, or HSRIC, and

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:34.060
I am specifically interested in promoting
the research support that this portal provides.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:40.430
HSRIC has a page specifically on Domestic
Violence, aimed at providing information on

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:44.590
this topic to assist researchers,
policy makers, and practitioners.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.140
I propose you use this resource to engage
with faculty and students through the development

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:54.400
of a workshop or supplemental day of
lecture and discussion for the classroom.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.600
You can access this resource by visiting the Education section of

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:03.360
the "Confronting Violence" website, and
clicking on Related Resources at NLM.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:08.540
Under the Health Services Research resource
side tab, and past the introduction,

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:10.260
there will be a bank of links.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:15.020
Click on the link below the Domestic Violence
HSR Topic Page.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:19.440
Alternatively, you can also enter from the
main NLM website, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.080
Click on Health Services Research &amp; Public
Health below Research at NLM at the bottom middle of the page,

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.900
then HSR Information Central under Collaborative Projects.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:34.160
Finally, select Domestic Violence under HSR Topics.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.580
Once you are on the Domestic Violence page,

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:40.860
you will see that it is divided into numerous sections.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:46.540
These sections include pre-structured searches
on domestic violence within NLM resources;

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:52.190
news; data, tools, statistics; assessment
tools, guidelines, and publications;

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:55.990
education and training; 
meetings, conferences, and webinars;

00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:00.870
grants, funding, and fellowships; 
legislation; and key organizations.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.380
As mentioned on the page, the literature and resources

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:08.240
related to domestic violence are growing,
but can be difficult to locate.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:13.000
The goal of Health Services Research and
Public Health Information Programs

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.200
is to integrate resources into research and practice,

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:21.920
and for Health Services Research specifically
the goal is one of studying access, costs, and quality.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.700
Taken as a whole, I can’t think of anything
more critical for today’s students, and

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.730
your programming can connect them to this resource.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:34.460
Domestic violence began to be treated as a
significant health issue

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:40.300
when concerned and diligent parties
proved its severity with statistics and reports.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:45.040
My idea for the workshop or day of
supplementary curriculum is to keep this focus

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:49.160
on data, assessment, and screening tools,
as expressed on the page.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:53.760
Nursing students also need excellent training,
a focus of the page as well.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:10:00.820
Buy-in for this idea requires facilitation
and agreement amongst nursing school staff.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:05.280
Various stakeholders at the institution should
be involved before any planning is undertaken,

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.260
but once there is support, these same individuals
can be used for promotion and outreach.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:13.280
I also propose you invite a visiting counselor

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.940
to instruct and moderate discussion
amongst the students.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:20.940
The material for these discussions will come
from watching the older videos available on

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.060
the "Confronting Violence" website
within the Digital Gallery,

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.440
and students will engage with one another
and inform their future methods

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.880
with an observance of behavior
and practice both then and now.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:38.820
My second companion programming idea involves
introducing the public to personal stories

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:43.431
of domestic abuse in a way that will foster
communication and community, and encourage

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.109
the exploration of information resources.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:50.380
I propose that you consider both the perspective
of the health care professional

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:52.720
and the person who experienced the violence.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:57.700
I suggest planning a reception for the opening
of the "Confronting Violence" exhibit where

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:03.090
the public can attend, explore exhibit themes,
share experiences, and educate themselves

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:04.850
on resources available.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.460
People of all ages should be encouraged to attend.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.920
Promotion can be done through local libraries
and organizations that specialize in

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:14.300
domestic violence relief and services,

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:18.120
and the event can be advertised
on institutional social media.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:23.260
To ease into stories, reflect on the past
and present, and tie directly to the exhibit,

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:25.980
invite both retired and practicing local nurses.

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:29.160
They can provide personal stories of experience.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.460
Reach out through your Nursing Liaison 
Librarian if you have one,

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.380
or leverage your  institutional network.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:38.040
I recognize that this is a difficult topic, and that

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:42.200
not everyone feels comfortable
experiencing it or sharing in the same way.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:46.960
Provide a space and time for the public to
share their experiences as well, but include

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:51.670
a collective creative area where art and collage
can be worked on as a sort of therapy and

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:54.170
icebreaker amongst attendees.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:57.670
Set aside a space where these art pieces can
be displayed afterwards.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:02.860
Alongside this reception, it is important
to keep the public informed.

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:06.860
Not everyone knows where to access information
relating to domestic violence.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:12.100
For this reason, I want you to direct the
public’s attention to the up-to-date, credible,

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:14.170
and linked information of MedlinePlus.

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:20.500
MedlinePlus is NLM's consumer health information
service, and it provides encyclopedic pages

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:25.980
on many health-related topics, collected from
NLM and across trusted health organizations,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:29.640
and the information is available in
both English and Spanish.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.700
MedlinePlus has a page on
domestic violence specifically.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.360
Access this resource by visiting the main
NLM website and

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:42.720
clicking on MedlinePlus under Databases, or
visit MedlinePlus.gov directly.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:46.420
Click on either of the two Health Topics icons
in the upper left.

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:48.980
From there, you can either search alphabetically

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:52.060
by clicking on D and scrolling to Domestic Violence,

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:55.800
or by clicking on Social/Family
Issues under Health and Wellness

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:57.520
and scrolling to Domestic Violence.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:02.840
Once you are on the page, you will clearly
see how its components have been structured.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:08.320
To connect to this resource while at the reception,
computers or tablets should be provided to

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:13.250
make this resource accessible, with staff
on hand to assist with the provided technology

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:18.790
or if attendees require help accessing MedlinePlus
on their personal mobile devices.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:23.290
Additionally, you may want to complement the
reception and the ensuing months of hosting

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:28.070
with a local display of items from your own
special collections or archives.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:33.320
My third companion programming idea involves

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:36.960
a cross-organizational and cross-disciplinary
journal club.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.360
Domestic violence is a topic that affects
so many different individuals, and bridges

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:44.110
across and beyond the health sciences.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:48.550
By partnering with community organizations
in your area and involving different schools,

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:53.360
departments, or units, each can lend a different
perspective and expertise when engaged in

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:55.140
conversation with others.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:00.300
The academic and the practical can meet here
to explore the impact of domestic violence

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:03.540
and how best to respond and care for those
who experience it.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:10.880
The companion web page to the sixth "Confronting
Violence" banner titled “And the Work Continues”

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:15.190
notes that activists today have developed
innovative approaches to sharing information

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:16.850
and assisting in recovery.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:21.440
While the exhibit mostly examines domestic
violence as it relates to women in general,

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:26.240
there is an increased focus on the specific
needs of teens, immigrant women,

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:29.800
women of color, and the role of men
in stopping violence.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:33.080
Use these ideas as a springboard,
and consider as well

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:37.840
how this issue affects men, trans, and
non-cisgender individuals differently.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:41.180
Make your institution and the traveling exhibit

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:44.420
the focal point for the first meeting,
and a place to gather.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:49.380
Shift to an online forum if you feel that
will keep and encourage participation.

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:55.280
Use institutional social media, websites,
and organization and liaison contacts to advertise.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:00.360
When selecting your journal club readings,
leverage the free and full-text journal articles

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:04.360
on Domestic Violence via
PubMed Central, or PMC.

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:10.380
PMC is the digital counterpart to NLM’s
print journal collection.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:16.220
It is an archive of scholarly biomedical and
life sciences articles that are free, full-text,

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:18.040
and publicly available online.

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:22.840
You can access a selection of these writings
by visiting the Education section of

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:27.680
the "Confronting Violence" website, and
clicking on Related Resources at NLM.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:33.060
Under the PubMed Central resource side tab,
you will see sixteen articles listed, with

00:15:33.061 --> 00:15:35.601
links directly to the item within PMC.

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:41.480
One example article is titled “Barriers
to Screening for Domestic Violence,” from

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:44.070
the Journal of General Internal Medicine.

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:48.860
This is a curated list, and a great place
to start, but there are many more articles

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:54.090
available via PMC using MeSH or
generic domestic violence search terms.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:16:00.040
PMC for search purposes can be accessed by
starting at the main NLM website, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/,

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:04.490
and selecting PubMed/MEDLINE in
the upper left under Databases.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:08.810
Either select PubMed Central under Popular
at the bottom middle of the page, or

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:13.160
enter your term into the search bar and then
limit your search by PMC.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:20.630
PMC can also be accessed from the main NLM
website by clicking on All NLM Databases &amp; APIs

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:26.170
in the upper left under Databases, and then
scrolling to PubMed Central (PMC).

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:36.240
In a 2014 Medical Library Association oral
history, past NLM Director Dr. Donald Lindberg

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:39.839
noted the successes of the Exhibition Program.

00:16:39.839 --> 00:16:43.990
He found it striking that every traveling
exhibit is amplified and made unique with

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:48.160
the things that each hosting institution is
most proud of.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:52.110
In addition to the ideas I covered today,
I invite you to think like Lindberg as you

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:56.870
plan for hosting, and look to the items and
partnerships that you are most proud of.

00:16:56.870 --> 00:17:01.691
Consider highlighting these alongside the
"Confronting Violence" exhibit, and use them

00:17:01.691 --> 00:17:05.240
as inspiration for activities and programming.

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:09.819
This is what will set you apart and lead to
a successful and engaging exhibition.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:17.520
Before closing, I want to thank all of the
individuals who responded to my call for comments

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:21.959
on "Confronting Violence," and for my colleagues
at NLM for their support.

00:17:21.959 --> 00:17:25.519
You were all instrumental in guiding my development
of this project.

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:32.920
Please reach out to Jiwon and I with your
thoughts, constructive comments, and any programming

00:17:32.929 --> 00:17:34.780
success stories you care to share.

